# Three.js Scene Graph Parsing

## Overview
Three.js organizes 3D objects in a hierarchical scene graph. Understanding this structure is essential for extracting parts and meshes from a scene.

## Key Concepts

### Object Hierarchy
- **THREE.Scene / THREE.Group**: Container nodes that group child objects. They have no geometry themselves but define a local coordinate system.
- **THREE.Mesh**: A leaf node (or intermediate) that holds geometry + material. Each mesh has a `.geometry` property (BufferGeometry) and `.position`, `.rotation`, `.quaternion`, `.scale` transforms.

### Traversing the Scene Graph
```javascript
// Traverse all descendants
root.traverse((child) => {
    if (child.isMesh) {
        console.log(child.name, child.geometry.type);
    }
    if (child.isGroup) {
        console.log('Group:', child.name);
    }
});

// Direct children only
root.children.forEach(child => { ... });
```

### World Transform vs Local Transform
- `child.position` / `child.rotation` / `child.scale` are **local** transforms relative to parent.
- `child.updateMatrixWorld(true)` computes the full world matrix.
- `child.matrixWorld` gives the 4x4 world transform after calling the above.

### Applying World Transform to Geometry
To export a mesh in world coordinates (so it appears in its assembled position):
```javascript
child.updateMatrixWorld(true);
const geom = child.geometry.clone();
geom.applyMatrix4(child.matrixWorld);
```

### Identifying Parts
- A "part" is typically a `THREE.Group` with a `.name`.
- Individual meshes directly under a group are sub-components of that part.
- A "link" is the merged geometry of all meshes within one part group.

## Common Geometry Types
- `BoxGeometry(width, height, depth)`
- `CylinderGeometry(radiusTop, radiusBottom, height, radialSegments)`
- `TorusGeometry(radius, tube, radialSegments, tubularSegments)`
- `ConeGeometry(radius, height, radialSegments)`
- `SphereGeometry(radius, widthSegments, heightSegments)`

## Installation
```bash
npm install three
```

## Usage Pattern for Headless Extraction
Three.js can be used headlessly in Node.js without a browser/WebGL context. Geometry creation and transforms work without a renderer.

# OBJ Export from Three.js Geometries

## Overview
The OBJ file format is a simple text-based 3D geometry format. This skill covers how to export Three.js BufferGeometry objects to `.obj` files.

## OBJ Format Structure
```
# Comment line
v x y z        # Vertex position
vn nx ny nz    # Vertex normal (optional)
f v1 v2 v3     # Face (1-indexed vertex indices)
f v1//n1 v2//n2 v3//n3  # Face with normals
```

## Using OBJExporter from Three.js addons

Three.js provides an `OBJExporter` in `three/examples/jsm/exporters/OBJExporter.js`:

```javascript
import { OBJExporter } from 'three/examples/jsm/exporters/OBJExporter.js';

const exporter = new OBJExporter();
const objString = exporter.parse(mesh); // Pass a Mesh or Scene
fs.writeFileSync('output.obj', objString);
```

## Manual OBJ Export (More Control)

For finer control, manually extract vertices and faces from BufferGeometry:

```javascript
function geometryToOBJ(geometry, name = 'object') {
    const position = geometry.getAttribute('position');
    const normal = geometry.getAttribute('normal');
    const index = geometry.getIndex();

    let obj = `# ${name}\n`;
